  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Monday)\nIssued at 1212 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nTwo distinct jet streams are expected to be present across the CONUS \non Thursday; a subtropical jetstream extending from the Pacific to \nthe southern tip of the Baja California peninsula and into the Gulf \nCoast of the CONUS. Meanwhile, the polar jet stream will be present \nacross the Northern CONUS with a trough in the vicinity of the \nPacific Northwest. \n\nMost, if not all, of Thursday will be pleasant with temperatures in \nthe 70s to low-80s. Expect clouds to begin to move in from the south \nas a trough embedded in the subtropical jetstream draws rich \nmoisture from (what is expected to be by that time per the NHC) \nTropical Depression Vance into Texas. Rain chances will rise \nthroughout Thursday night as the trough tracks into southwest Texas \non a northeasterly trajectory. \n\nThe bulk of the forcing, thus the greatest rain chances, will be \nlate Friday morning but expect rain to continue through the day \nbefore gradually coming to an end from southwest to northeast Friday \nnight. PWATs are expected to be around 1\" to 1.50\" across the \nmajority of the combined Panhandles which is above the 95th \npercentile for late October and approaching climatological max \nvalues. This abundant moisture will favor light to moderate rainfall \nwith heavier showers and heavy rainfall with thunderstorms. A main \nlimiting factor for the potential for flash flooding is that most \nmodel guidance shows very weak instability (100-300 J/kg), \ndecreasing the chances for the widespread and persistent \nthunderstorms which produce the higher-end rain rates. Nonetheless, \nthere will still be a very low chance for flooding/flash flooding \ngiven the significant rainfall that we have observed over the past \n30-40 days. \n\nThe latest available LREF (27/00z) at the time of writing has a 60-\n90% chance for rain amounts to exceed 0.50\" across the southeastern \nhalf of the combined Panhandles, and a 30-60% chance to exceed 1\" \nfor the same area. Beware: a number of factors are still very much \nin play which includes the track and strength of the trough. If it \nspeeds up (remains more of an open wave), we will likely see the \nrain amounts decrease. If it tracks more to the east, we can expect \nthe higher rain amounts to move with it. The more likely scenario \nbetween the two at this time appears to be that the trough could \nspeed up and lessen rain amounts, but that's a roughly 30% chance \nright now.\n\nDry conditions will return for Saturday and early Sunday before yet \nanother weather system may work in from the Pacific Northwest and re-\nintroduces rain chances. Early indications are that this has the \npotential to be quite a dynamic weather system with strong winds.\n\nVanden Bosch",
